I’ll first inventory the measurement, metadata, reusable refinement scripts, and candidate-source index. Then I’ll identify a minimal physically plausible phase set, health-screen every CIF before fitting, establish a replay-stable GSAS-II checkpoint, and only then run focused profile/texture/source challenges before freezing the deliverables.

The chemistry strongly suggests Sr–W–O products, but at 650 °C several stoichiometries/polymorphs and unreacted precursors are plausible. I’m treating that as context only: next I’m extracting the observed peak families and the candidate database schema, while checking the installed indexing/refinement interfaces so formula balance does not prejudge the diffraction evidence.

The dominant peaks are not explained by a single obvious precursor fingerprint, and the supplied database contains only one exact Sr–W–O record (Sr₃W₂O₉). I’m now comparing that record with charge-balanced Sr–W–O topologies (SrWO₄, Sr₂WO₅, Sr₃WO₆, SrW₂O₇, etc.) through several chemically sensible analogue parents, while keeping WO₃ and SrCO₃ as explicit source competitors.

The analogue inventory yields a strong, chemically justified SrWO₄ candidate via the isostructural SrMoO₄ scheelite record, plus a lower-symmetry ABO₄ competitor; other non‑1:1 stoichiometries have much weaker or chemically remote parents, except the exact Sr₃W₂O₉ record. I’m health-screening these serious topology candidates and all exact precursor polymorphs before any Rietveld fitting, then scoring their independent peak-family coverage.

The peak-family test now clearly supports scheelite SrWO₄ as the dominant phase (including the high-angle Kα₁/Kα₂ doublets), while residual families independently match hexagonal WO₃ and orthorhombic SrCO₃. The exact Sr₃W₂O₉ record has several decisive low-angle/position mismatches, so it remains a challenge model rather than part of the initial inventory. I’m building the first closed three-phase GSAS-II model with all inactive size/strain/texture/roughness terms set literally, then I’ll inspect residual peaks before considering any expansion.

The first three-phase branch is invalid: the SrCO₃ scale crossed zero and hit its bound, then its unconstrained cell became singular. Per the gate, I’m discarding that comparison rather than interpreting it. I’m reloading from clean values and converging the physically supported SrWO₄ + hex‑WO₃ core first; only after that checkpoint will SrCO₃ be reintroduced at a scale commensurate with its calculated intensity and tested for positive, replay-stable support.

The replay-stable two-phase checkpoint is now valid (no frozen parameters), but its WO₃ Bragg residual is very poor and the unexplained 22.95/23.83/24.65° triplet is characteristic of lower-symmetry WO₃. I’m therefore applying the required “poor record ≠ rejected chemistry” test: monoclinic, triclinic, tetragonal, and orthorhombic WO₃ records are being given the same neutral background/cell/shift/common-width treatment before any additional formula is admitted.

The matched source test identifies orthorhombic Pnma WO₃ as the best physical record so far (neutral \(wR=8.79\%\)), ahead of monoclinic (9.63%), tetragonal (10.85%), hexagonal (12.17%), and triclinic (20.81%). All retained scales are positive and no terms are frozen. I’m now independently replaying the orthorhombic checkpoint to remove the remaining large shift/esd, then I’ll give the orthorhombic and monoclinic leaders the same compact texture basis before selecting topology.

The compact texture screen gives a clear, physically moderate pair for the orthorhombic model: SrWO₄ [110] (March 0.707) and WO₃ [001] (1.490). The monoclinic competitor only approaches this by driving WO₃ texture to 4.31 with 99.7% scale correlation (or to a singular ratio of 12.6), so those branches are not reliable. I’m combining the retained leaders and cross-seeding the same SrWO₄ nuisance term into the best valid monoclinic branch; then I’ll perform neutral deletions of each texture term.

The combined texture model is replay-stable and both texture deletions worsen \(wR\) materially (to 7.69% and 8.02%), so both compact terms are retained. The remaining residuals are largely derivative-shaped around established families, not yet clean evidence for a new phase. I’m now doing the required phase-by-phase neutral/size/strain/combined profile tests; only coherent residual families left after that challenge can justify a third phase or same-formula coexistence.

The first profile-width sweep exposed invalid branches: SrWO₄ broadening either hit the size limit or demanded negative strain, while WO₃ size collapsed. I’m correcting the inactive-term initialization and rerunning the comparison so only physically valid, replay-stable branches can influence the final model.

The corrected sweep is decisive: only orthorhombic WO₃ isotropic strain is physical and stable (Rwp 6.21% versus 6.57% neutral). SrWO₄ strain goes negative, and both size branches hit limits or collapse, so I’m pruning those terms and carrying forward only the WO₃ strain checkpoint. Next I’m challenging the remaining residuals against plausible extra phases and the lower-symmetry SrWO₄ topology.

A third phase is now unambiguous: orthorhombic SrCO₃ refines to about 15 wt% with a large Rwp drop (6.21% → 5.15%) and a stable positive scale. Sr₃W₂O₉ only improves after its cell distorts drastically from the source metric, so that branch is not physically admissible. A second WO₃ topology also gains scale, which triggers the stricter same-formula coexistence tests rather than immediate retention.

The same-formula test remains credible after adding SrCO₃: both WO₃ models retain strong positive scales (about 7% orthorhombic and 13% monoclinic), and the four-phase fit reaches Rwp 4.06% after compact texture tests. I’m now repeating neutral/size/strain/combined profile challenges for all four retained phases at this mature inventory; terms that bind, go negative, or trade excessively will be removed.

The strict coexistence deletion test passed cleanly. From the same mature checkpoint, suppressing orthorhombic WO₃ raises Rwp to 4.77%, and suppressing monoclinic WO₃ raises it to 5.10%; both surviving single-WO₃ branches remain stable. The retained pair also has noncoincident families (for example, monoclinic 22.51°/41.33° and orthorhombic 47.48°/54.07°). I’m pruning texture and then testing background/polarization/roughness before freezing outputs.

The angular-trend tests support a small roughness correction, while free polarization becomes redundant. I’m pruning to the six-term smooth background, replaying the entire measured range, and then freezing/exporting all artifacts from that same project.

The full-range project converged twice after independent reopen/replay at Rwp 3.475%, with all four scales positive. I’m now health-checking the exported final CIFs and auditing that the project, fractions, profile, and reported statistics are mutually consistent.

Completed a replay-stable four-phase refinement. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material.

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Scheelite SrWO₄ | 63.37% |
| Orthorhombic WO₃ | 5.73% |
